  <section class="hero ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Glossary</p>
      <h1>The words this market uses.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Thirty-three terms you will meet in a data licence or a diligence call, defined in plain English. Where a word is doing legal work, the definition says so.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Rights and ownership</h2>
      <dl class="glossary reveal">
        <dt>Assignment</dt><dd>The transfer of ownership itself, as opposed to a licence to use something. A sale, in this market, is usually an assignment or an exclusive licence dressed as one.</dd>
        <dt>Work made for hire</dt><dd>A U.S. copyright concept: work created by an employee in the course of employment belongs to the employer. It is why your team&rsquo;s records usually start in your hands, and why contractor work needs its own check.</dd>
        <dt>Licence versus sale</dt><dd>A licence grants defined rights for a defined term; ownership stays with you. A sale transfers the rights. Most AI data deals are licences, which is what lets a seller license the same records twice.</dd>
        <dt>Scope</dt><dd>What is licensed: which records, which years, which fields, which systems, and which categories are excluded. It is the first thing to negotiate and the one that changes the price most.</dd>
        <dt>Permitted use</dt><dd>What the buyer may do with the data. Training, evaluation, benchmarking and internal research are different permissions, and a buyer will often want the broadest.</dd>
        <dt>Field of use</dt><dd>A limit on the market or application the data may be used in, such as customer support software but not recruiting tools. It preserves room to license elsewhere.</dd>
        <dt>Exclusivity</dt><dd>A promise not to license the same records to anyone else for a period. It is the most valuable thing a seller gives away, and it should be priced as such.</dd>
        <dt>Sublicence</dt><dd>A licence granted by the buyer to a third party under its own licence. If it is not permitted in writing, the buyer cannot pass your data on.</dd>
        <dt>Onward transfer</dt><dd>The onward movement of data to another party, including a buyer&rsquo;s customer or a model provider. Ask where the data ends up, not only who receives it first.</dd>
        <dt>Term</dt><dd>How long the licence runs, and what happens at the end: renewal, deletion, or reversion of rights.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Privacy and personal data</h2>
      <dl class="glossary reveal">
        <dt>Personal data</dt><dd>Information relating to an identifiable person. The test is identifiability, not whether a name is present.</dd>
        <dt>Anonymisation</dt><dd>Processing that makes re-identification not reasonably likely, considering who might attempt it and with what. It is a standard to be met, not a formatting step.</dd>
        <dt>Pseudonymisation</dt><dd>Replacing identifiers with codes so the data cannot be attributed without extra information. Pseudonymised data is still personal data.</dd>
        <dt>De-identification</dt><dd>The general term for removing or transforming identifiers. It covers everything from stripping names to rewriting free text, and the result varies enormously.</dd>
        <dt>Re-identification</dt><dd>Recovering the identity of a person from data that was supposed to be anonymous, often by combining it with other data.</dd>
        <dt>Direct identifier</dt><dd>Something that names a person outright: a name, email address, phone number, account number.</dd>
        <dt>Quasi-identifier</dt><dd>Something that identifies only in combination: job title, employer, city, dates, an unusual sequence of events.</dd>
        <dt>Data processing agreement</dt><dd>The contract that governs how one party handles personal data for another. In a licensing deal it sits alongside the licence, not instead of it.</dd>
        <dt>Controller and processor</dt><dd>The party that decides why and how personal data is processed, and the party that processes it on the controller&rsquo;s behalf. Which one a buyer is affects what it may do with your records.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Deal mechanics</h2>
      <dl class="glossary reveal">
        <dt>Letter of intent</dt><dd>A short statement of proposed terms, usually non-binding. The one we work with runs 14 days and can be extended by seven, which is what makes it safe to sign.</dd>
        <dt>Term sheet</dt><dd>A more detailed summary of commercial terms, still usually non-binding, used before the full agreement is drafted.</dd>
        <dt>Audit right</dt><dd>Your right to inspect something, typically a sample of the de-identified data or the buyer&rsquo;s calculation of what you are owed. Worth having in writing.</dd>
        <dt>Deletion certificate</dt><dd>Written confirmation that a copy of the data was destroyed. Rare in this market; a deletion period is common, evidence of it much less so.</dd>
        <dt>Manifest</dt><dd>The inventory that travels with a delivery: what was sent, how many records, what was removed. An incomplete manifest is one of the most common reasons payment slips.</dd>
        <dt>Most-favoured-nation</dt><dd>A clause promising you terms at least as good as those given to someone else. Useful in theory, hard to police in practice.</dd>
        <dt>Renewal</dt><dd>Whether the licence continues automatically, must be renegotiated, or ends. Check it before you sign, because it decides whether the price is one payment or a stream.</dd>
        <dt>Indemnity</dt><dd>A promise to cover someone&rsquo;s loss if a claim arises. Sellers are usually asked to warrant their rights; read what you are being asked to cover.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Records and market terms</h2>
      <dl class="glossary reveal">
        <dt>Corpus</dt><dd>The body of records offered as a dataset. Coherence matters more than size: a consistent corpus of one kind of work is easier to price than a pile of everything.</dd>
        <dt>Full corpus</dt><dd>A buyer&rsquo;s term for licensing the whole body of records it has reviewed, rather than a sample or a slice. Several programmes only pay a fee on a full-corpus licence.</dd>
        <dt>Provenance</dt><dd>The documented history of a dataset: where it came from, who holds the rights, how it was processed, and what restrictions apply.</dd>
        <dt>Rights file</dt><dd>The document that records provenance and the rights position for one dataset. We publish the twelve fields and a template on <a href="rights-file.html">the rights file page</a>.</dd>
        <dt>Data card</dt><dd>A short public description of a dataset: contents, size, date range, collection method, known gaps and restrictions. Related to a datasheet, which is the longer research version.</dd>
        <dt>Unit rate</dt><dd>A price per item, such as per article, image or audio hour. It prices a component, not a corpus, and it is a floor rather than a quote.</dd>
      </dl>
